A||||6||ChadMan||Here's a question for the Mister Two guys||||5||
Z||000000||ChadMan||12-01-2000||11:06 AM||||Assuming both are normally aspirated and neither had been modded, which is faster - a 1st gen (flying-wedge) MR2 or a 2nd gen MR2?  I have driven one of each (an 86 and a 91) and the 86 felt faster.<P>Incidentally, I have stoplight-raced several MR2's (1st gen and 2nd gen, but never a Turbo or S/C).  I never lost in my 86 SE V6 and never won in my 88 4-banger.<P>Thanks! (Edited to remove model years...I wasn't sure which year the MR2 changed body styles)<p>[This message has been edited by ChadMan (edited 12-01-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||PearlBlueSoul||12-01-2000||04:04 PM||||First gen MR2s are slower than second gen Mr2s in either n/a or 'charged form.  the S/Cs can beat 2nd gen n/as but not turbos. <P>That said, the real competition you're looking for is a (as you put it) Supercharged "flying wedge".   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  If you ever race a modded turbo motor, I guarantee you stock to stock you would lose, quite badly I might add.<P>if you raced a modded V6 versus a modded Turbo, you would lose, prolly the same to a modded s/c depending on what all had been done.  Remember:  MR2s in general are very light, and the way toyota manufactured the motors in these cars left much room for modifications and improvements to be made.  And to put some scope on relations, basicaly the MR2 turbo motor is a Supra motor of the same year, minus 2 cylinders.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
Z||000002||VelociFero||12-01-2000||11:11 PM||||As for "feeling" faster:  The MKIs, especially the earlier ones,  are known as Road Karts.  They have a... tossability (for lack of a better word) that was traded off for acceleration and comfort as the years went on.  The newest MkIIIs have returned to the original formula, though, and they're a BLAST to drive.||||reg||1
Z||000003||JasonMr2||12-02-2000||01:51 AM||||My '91 N/A MR2 is just about neck and neck with my friends 86 Fiero GT.  I think I have just a slight advantage over him cause of my 5-speed vs. his auto.  Neither one of our cars have any mods but his engine was just rebuilt earlier this year. BTW '91 was the first year of the 2nd gen||||reg||1
Z||000004||Slammed Fiero||12-04-2000||11:35 AM||||My friend has an 88 SC MR2, the car has the japenese spec motor which he says puts out 160hp , My 86 GT with minimal mods is faster (not by much). I can pull consistent 6.58 sec to 60 in the car (ask fierolisa she is witness as is Chris moore (GTEC)). As for the turbo's being a lot faster, I found that on the track the MR2 turbo never got into the boost enough to pull away , this is a tight track. I found the car that posed the biggest threat at an autocross was an 84 MR2 , it weighs 2021lbs , as apose to the 88 MR2 SC which weighed in at 2700 , even the 1985  cars weighed 300lbs more. While the 84 at the autocross was slower than the SC at the same event , the 84 was Totally stock , the 88SC has been lowered , bushings all done , big bar in the rear. Lots of stuff.<P>My car is a 4spd , Chris moore of Northstar fame (he has 2 N* cars) also has an 88 5spd with a 3.4 , it will pull 7.0sec flat to sixty ..go 4spd..haha<P>I have yet to Drive an MR2 , I have ridden in 2 early cars , the SC one mentioned above and another 85 N/A. Both nice cars , you can feel the weight difference in the cars , but you can also feel the torque (or lack there of) just a different powerband really. Last Autocross I was at the Difference between my 86 GT that has coil overs , koni's and lots of other stuff , and my friends 88SC with the smae type of mods (tokico's (5way) bars , lowered ect) was 1.2 seconds. His main problems were the brakes on the front locking up , as were mine (going to 12" discs all around) All in all it's good fun to battle with them.<P><BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>coil overs , Eibachs in rear , Carrera springs in front , Big bars , 17" Tr motorsports typhoons , Cold air induction , accel coil , no cat , 50,000 original miles..||||reg||1
Z||000005||PearlBlueSoul||12-04-2000||03:10 PM||||double post<p>[This message has been edited by PearlBlueSoul (edited 12-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||PearlBlueSoul||12-04-2000||03:10 PM||||yah, that's one thing I do hate abou the MR2s is that the rear end isn't very motivated to get involved in braking issues.  I'm looking into a quad piston caliper setup with a new bias spring so I can tune in some rear brake bias.  I hate having my front wheels choke when I want to stop.  9 times out of 10 I find myself pulling up on the e-brake for a more balanced stop.<BR>||||reg||1
